1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:05,360
Picture trying to coach someone through fixing a sprawling software project entirely over text message.

2
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:13,284
You can describe what to do, but you can't see the screen, you can't run the tests, and you can't remember what you said three messages ago.

3
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:19,748
That, in essence, is the predicament of a large language model trying to act as an autonomous agent.

4
00:00:20,014 --> 00:00:25,860
And the survey we're walking through today argues that the answer to this predicament is hiding in plain sight.

5
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:34,480
Code itself, not as a thing the model produces, but as the operational scaffolding that lets it act, observe, and remember.

6
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:50,560
The paper is titled Code as Agent Harness, and it offers a unified survey and conceptual framing for how code has quietly evolved from being the output of language models into being the medium through which agentic systems actually function.

7
00:00:50,990 --> 00:01:07,716
The authors borrow a term from the systems community, the agent harness, which refers to the software layer, wrapping a language model with tools, sandboxes, memory stores, validators, permission boundaries, execution loops, and feedback channels.

8
00:01:08,526 --> 00:01:21,476
Their core claim is that the bottleneck for autonomous agents is no longer just raw reasoning ability, but the reliability of the harness that connects model outputs to long-running actions and persistent state.

9
00:01:22,222 --> 00:01:32,644
And code, they argue, is uniquely suited to be the substrate of that harness, because it is simultaneously executable, inspectable, and stateful.

10
00:01:33,454 --> 00:01:38,308
To understand why this matters, consider how prior work has typically used code.

11
00:01:39,118 --> 00:01:53,220
Program-aided reasoning approaches like Program of Thoughts or PAL recognize that language models are unreliable at arithmetic and symbolic manipulation, so they delegate computation to a Python interpreter.

12
00:01:53,550 --> 00:02:00,452
The model writes a program, the interpreter runs it, and the answer comes back grounded in real execution.

13
00:02:00,846 --> 00:02:08,260
Embodied agents like SACAN or CODAS policies use generated programs as robot control policies.

14
00:02:08,654 --> 00:02:17,028
Software engineering benchmarks like SWE bench treat repositories, tests, and execution traces as the environment.

15
00:02:17,518 --> 00:02:23,360
Each of these is a slice of a broader pattern, but the field has lacked a unifying frame.

16
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:29,348
The authors offer that frame by organizing the entire landscape into three connected layers.

17
00:02:29,806 --> 00:02:35,396
The first layer is the harness interface, which is where code first enters the agent loop.

18
00:02:35,694 --> 00:02:37,760
Here code plays three roles.

19
00:02:38,094 --> 00:02:47,460
It serves as a reasoning substrate, externalizing internal computation into programs that interpreters or formal verifiers can check.

20
00:02:47,886 --> 00:02:55,620
Think of this as the difference between doing arithmetic in your head versus writing it on paper where someone can audit each step.

21
00:02:56,110 --> 00:03:09,796
It serves as an action interface, translating high-level intent into executable operations, whether that means robot motion commands, GUI clicks grounded in DOM trees, or API calls.

22
00:03:10,318 --> 00:03:22,756
And it serves as an environment model where repositories, simulators, execution traces, and tests become the persistent, queryable representation of the world the agent operates in.

23
00:03:23,502 --> 00:03:26,756
The crucial insight is that code is not metaphorical here.

24
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:36,160
The authors explicitly bound the term to mean executable or machine checkable artifacts, distinguishing it from raw perception or latent model reasoning.

25
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:45,764
The second layer is harness mechanisms, and this is where the survey gets into the operational machinery that keeps agents reliable over long horizons.

26
00:03:47,086 --> 00:03:54,880
Planning is reframed not as an internal model capability, but as a harness control problem, with four flavors.

27
00:03:55,246 --> 00:03:59,920
Linear decomposition, where the agent commits to a single ordered plan;

28
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:06,628
structure-grounded planning that anchors decisions in dependency graphs or repository structure;

29
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:14,080
search-based planning that explores multiple trajectories using Monte Carlo tree search or similar techniques;

30
00:04:14,382 --> 00:04:20,100
and orchestration-based planning, where specialized roles coordinate through staged workflows.

31
00:04:20,622 --> 00:04:35,108
Memory engineering becomes a state management layer that decides what stays in the active context window, what gets compacted into summaries, and what gets offloaded to external storage like vector databases or file system artifacts.

32
00:04:35,726 --> 00:04:54,660
A clever recent practice they highlight is treating PLAN files like plan.md or status logs as persistent harness objects, version controlled and inspectable, so that an agent can survive context resets the way a software engineer survives going home for the night and coming back the next morning.

33
00:04:55,150 --> 00:05:07,600
Tool use, control loops that plan, execute, and verify against deterministic sensors and even harness self-improvement through telemetry and evolution agents all live in this layer.

34
00:05:07,918 --> 00:05:11,440
The third layer scales the harness from one agent to many.

35
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:33,280
When multiple agents collaborate over a shared code base, the harness must coordinate roles like manager, planner, coder, reviewer, and tester, support interaction modes from cooperative programming to adversarial red teaming, and arrange them in topologies, ranging from centralized to distributed to streaming.

36
00:05:33,678 --> 00:05:46,788
The shared code artifacts, repositories, tests, and execution traces become the common workspace, much like how a Git repository serves as the source of truth for a human engineering team.

37
00:05:47,502 --> 00:05:56,004
The harness here is no longer just the runtime around one model, it's the social and technical fabric through which many models reach consensus.

38
00:05:56,686 --> 00:06:02,948
The authors then survey how this code as harness view manifests across application domains.

39
00:06:03,438 --> 00:06:08,420
Coding assistants, like Claude Code and Codex, use repositories as state.

40
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:17,124
GUI and OS agents ground actions in DOM trees and accessibility APIs with executable evaluators.

41
00:06:17,518 --> 00:06:24,720
Scientific discovery agents compose hypothesis testing pipelines spanning simulations and lab protocols.

42
00:06:25,070 --> 00:06:29,840
Embodied agents like Voyager build persistent skill libraries in Minecraft.

43
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:34,400
Personalization agents maintain executable user profiles.

44
00:06:34,734 --> 00:06:35,920
The pattern repeats.

45
00:06:36,206 --> 00:06:40,004
Code provides the inspectable, executable substrate.

46
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:43,840
Now for the limitations, the survey honestly surfaces.

47
00:06:44,110 --> 00:06:52,000
Evaluation is a significant open problem because most benchmarks measure final task success rather than harness quality.

48
00:06:52,398 --> 00:07:00,452
If a benchmark doesn't stress long-range dependency management, you can't tell whether planning improvements are real or artifacts of the test setup.

49
00:07:01,006 --> 00:07:08,160
Verification under incomplete feedback remains hard, because not every failure produces a clean runtime exception.

50
00:07:08,494 --> 00:07:12,480
A robot reaching past its workspace might just silently fail.

51
00:07:12,846 --> 00:07:21,120
Regression-free harness improvement is an unsolved problem, since evolving the harness itself risks breaking workflows that previously worked.

52
00:07:21,454 --> 00:07:27,040
Multi agent systems struggle with transactional shared state and semantic conflict resolution.

53
00:07:27,342 --> 00:07:35,040
Similar to the classic database consistency problem, but harder, because the conflicts can be semantic rather than syntactic.

54
00:07:35,918 --> 00:07:47,428
And human oversight for safety critical actions, multimodal extensions beyond text and code, and the lack of a real science of harness engineering all remain open frontiers.

55
00:07:47,822 --> 00:07:51,792
Now let's walk through some questions you might have after hearing all of this.

56
00:07:52,078 --> 00:07:57,152
First, why is code specifically better than natural language as a harness medium?

57
00:07:57,582 --> 00:08:00,644
The answer comes down to three properties that work together.

58
00:08:01,070 --> 00:08:10,500
Executability means the harness can actually verify what the model intended by running it and observing real consequences, not just paraphrasing it.

59
00:08:10,862 --> 00:08:18,500
Inspectability means failures produce traces and stack frames that the harness can read and feed back into the next reasoning step.

60
00:08:18,990 --> 00:08:28,112
And statefulness means the program itself, the repository, or the execution log persists across steps so the agent's progress isn't lost.

61
00:08:28,526 --> 00:08:31,300
Natural language gives you none of these reliably.

62
00:08:31,598 --> 00:08:37,476
You can describe an action in English, but you can't run it, you can't diff it, and you can't merge it.

63
00:08:37,902 --> 00:08:44,292
Second, what's actually new about framing things as a harness rather than just calling it agent infrastructure?

64
00:08:44,878 --> 00:08:51,872
The reframing matters because it shifts the locus of reliability away from the model and onto the system.

65
00:08:52,302 --> 00:09:03,712
If you believe the harness is the bottleneck, you stop trying to make the model smarter and start trying to make the surrounding scaffolding more robust, more inspectable, and more recoverable.

66
00:09:03,982 --> 00:09:06,832
It also unifies disparate research threads.

67
00:09:07,182 --> 00:09:23,556
Program-aided reasoning, robot policy synthesis, repository-level coding agents, and multi-agent debate frameworks all turn out to be instances of the same underlying pattern, which means insights from one domain can transfer to another.

68
00:09:24,206 --> 00:09:30,500
Third, how does dynamically generated code relate to the human-designed harness infrastructure?

69
00:09:30,990 --> 00:09:33,952
This is a subtle point the survey emphasizes.

70
00:09:34,350 --> 00:09:44,672
Agent-generated code, like a temporary script the agent writes to inspect a database or a reusable skill it adds to its library, does not replace the broader harness.

71
00:09:45,294 --> 00:09:54,180
The human-designed layer still owns the sandbox boundaries, the permission tiers, the verification oracles, and the human review gates.

72
00:09:54,512 --> 00:10:12,612
Generated code makes the harness more adaptive because the agent can reshape its execution environment around the current task, but the policy-governed system around it decides what code may be executed, trusted, or promoted into future workflows.

73
00:10:13,326 --> 00:10:22,468
Think of the agent as a contractor who can build tools on the job, but the building code, the inspections, and the permits are set by someone else.

74
00:10:23,246 --> 00:10:30,404
Now, how do these ideas land in the world of large-scale recommendation systems serving hundreds of millions of users?

75
00:10:30,862 --> 00:10:42,148
Consider that modern recommenders are increasingly moving toward agentic patterns where a language model orchestrates retrieval, ranking, explanation, and personalization.

76
00:10:42,734 --> 00:10:55,876
The code as harness view suggests treating user state, candidate pools, ranking signals, and policy constraints as executable, inspectable artifacts rather than opaque embeddings.

77
00:10:56,526 --> 00:11:12,420
A personalization agent could maintain a user's preference model as a versioned, queryable program rather than a fixed embedding, allowing for explicit edits, auditing, and rollback when preferences shift or a recommendation goes wrong.

78
00:11:13,198 --> 00:11:30,180
Tools like sandboxed execution and deterministic verification map directly onto the safety challenges of serving billions of impressions, where you need bright lines between what an agent may suggest, what it may A B test, and what it may push to production.

79
00:11:30,702 --> 00:11:42,592
More concretely, the orchestration-based planning patterns and multi-agent harnesses are highly relevant for candidate retrieval and ranking pipelines, where specialized agents could handle.

80
00:11:42,592 --> 00:11:52,112
recall, re-rank, diversity, and policy compliance, coordinating through shared executable state, like feature stores or candidate logs.

81
00:11:52,398 --> 00:12:05,220
The harness telemetry idea, treating execution traces as the substrate for harness improvement, maps onto the offline replay and counterfactual evaluation infrastructure that recommender teams already build.

82
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:22,116
And the open problem of regression free harness improvement is essentially the same problem as safely evolving a ranking system without degrading user metrics, suggesting that techniques developed for code agents could feed back into the experimentation discipline that production recommenders rely on.

